WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.228 --> 00:00:01.521
(bright music)

00:00:01.521 --> 00:00:02.790
- [Narrator] The great British love affair

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:06.273
with train travel is as old
as the railways themselves.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.620
From the beating heart
of London's metropolis

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.800
to the majesty of the Scottish Highlands,

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:18.240
connecting coast and countryside
with our great cities,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.269
the East Coast line has it all.

00:00:21.269 --> 00:00:23.040
(record scratching)
(bell clanging)

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.720
- Disregard that. It's a glitch.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.430
- [Narrator] But it's
the people who give life

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:27.300
to this railway.
(whistle blasts)

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.520
- I won't frisk you. I trust you.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.637
- British public are the best form

00:00:30.637 --> 00:00:32.190
of entertainment in the planet.

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:33.523
(hen party cheering)

00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:34.356
(whistle blows)

00:00:34.356 --> 00:00:36.000
- [Narrator] The East Coast line carries

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.030
19 million passengers a year,

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:42.870
on 155 train journeys a day,

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.690
along almost 1000 miles of track.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:49.080
The East Coast is the
largest operator on the line.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.680
It runs trains, manages stations,

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.560
and employs nearly 3000 staff.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.270
- That's all you're doing all day.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:58.980
- I'm not playing with you
while you're showing off.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.332
- [Narrator] The East Coast
workforce toils tirelessly-

00:01:02.332 --> 00:01:05.006
- Mind your backs, please!
- from driving trains-

00:01:05.006 --> 00:01:07.080
- Look at this office window. Fantastic.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.400
- [Narrator] to making breakfasts...

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.080
- I've cooked that much
bacon over the years.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.820
I'm surprised I haven't turned into a pig.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.950
- [Narrator] to controlling
the whole train set.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.080
- It's boarding. That's
absolutely excellent, Graham.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.730
- [Narrator] Keeping everything on track

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.920
behind the scenes and in public.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.450
- I've been standing
here for half an hour.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.378
- [Narrator] These are the
real heroes of the railway.

00:01:24.378 --> 00:01:26.228
(whistle blows)
(upbeat rock music)

00:01:26.228 --> 00:01:30.028
(horn blaring)
(train whooshing)

00:01:30.028 --> 00:01:31.200
(bright music)

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.350
East Coast Trains is a huge operation.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:38.180
Behind the scenes, a dedicated
workforce grafts 24/7

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:39.960
to keep everything on track.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.127
- We are the unsung heroes of the railway.

00:01:43.127 --> 00:01:44.490
- [Narrator] And on the front line

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.166
is an army of devoted ambassadors-

00:01:47.166 --> 00:01:48.330
(door thuds)
- I'll put your suitcase in,

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:49.740
just so you're not standing.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.243
- [Narrator] From whistleblowers-

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:52.350
(whistle blows)

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:53.748
to breakfast chefs.

00:01:53.748 --> 00:01:55.560
- Ah me bacon.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:56.820
- [Narrator] These guys and gals are

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.650
the public face of the railway,

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:02.010
and they're primed for every eventuality.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.566
- Apparently there's
some suspicious youths

00:02:03.566 --> 00:02:05.100
just up round the cycle racks.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.230
- Ah look, your coat's all undone.

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.840
- [Narrator] Highly trained
to deal with the best-

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.240
- If anybody wants the
paper I'll leave it here.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.860
- [Narrator] And the worst that we,

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.903
Joe Public, can throw at them.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.118
- If you did this on an airline,

00:02:18.118 --> 00:02:20.520
you'd get to your destination
and you'd be arrested.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.880
- [Narrator] It's their job
to keep calm and carry on.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.350
- They reckon there's
a total loss of power.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.690
But being a Friday it couldn't get worse.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.814
- [Narrator] Through good times and bad.

00:02:30.814 --> 00:02:33.236
(staff laughing)

00:02:33.236 --> 00:02:35.277
(babbling nonsensically)

00:02:35.277 --> 00:02:36.427
(door clunks)

00:02:36.427 --> 00:02:38.786
- That's the last train I'll
ever dispatch in my life.

00:02:38.786 --> 00:02:42.246
(hen party cheering)

00:02:42.246 --> 00:02:44.587
(train rumbling)

00:02:44.587 --> 00:02:45.420
(horn hoots)

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.180
- I had one guy who said,
"Can I have a poached egg?"

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.430
And I said, "No you
can't have a poached egg,

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.590
I says because we've only got omelettes.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.030
I do apologise, sir."

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.530
And he went, "No, no."

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.000
He says, "I don't think you understand.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:57.930
Can I have a poached egg?"

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.930
And I went, "I don't know
what you don't understand

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.850
that I've only got omelettes."

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.160
And I had to say it quite sternly.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.530
And he went, "Really?

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:10.140
Don't they come from the
same place of chicken?"

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.720
And I went, "Well they
do, but," (chuckles)

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.460
I says, "they're not made on a train.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.477
So the omelettes are made
and then they're brought on

00:03:18.477 --> 00:03:20.280
and I regenerate them."

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:23.427
And he went, "So you
don't do poached eggs?"

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.078
And I just went: "No. No, mate no."

00:03:27.078 --> 00:03:30.161
(light blues music)

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.333
- [Narrator] All aboard the
full English breakfast club.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:44.010
Dishing up over 16,000 fry-ups
a year are top chef, Mark

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:46.473
and customer service assistant, Karen.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:50.603
- Morning.

00:03:50.603 --> 00:03:51.703
Did you place an order
with the chef this morning?

00:03:51.703 --> 00:03:53.730
- I did.
- What did you order?

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:57.404
There we go. And for you, sir.
- Thank you.

00:03:57.404 --> 00:03:58.313
- Alright, alright, whoa!

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.330
Any tomato sauce or brown sauce?

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:04.810
Thank you very much, cheers.

00:04:08.672 --> 00:04:11.505
(sanguine music)

00:04:17.026 --> 00:04:20.359
(ticker board clacking)

00:04:22.573 --> 00:04:25.380
It's 04:20 in the morning! (laughs)

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:26.970
Mark is always very tired at this point

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:28.793
and I'm always very alive.

00:04:30.026 --> 00:04:31.003
- Yeah.

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:33.027
- Morning, you alright?

00:04:33.027 --> 00:04:35.520
We're on the 045 from Newcastle

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.110
to London Kings Cross this morning.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.330
We stop at Durham,
Darlington, Northallerton,

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:43.860
York, Doncaster, Newark, North
Gate, Grantham, Peterborough,

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.240
and then London Kings Cross.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.863
So nine stops.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:49.650
Nine stops, yeah. Nine stops! (laughs)

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.887
And eight of them we pick
up and we do a service from.

00:04:52.887 --> 00:04:55.637
(vibrant music)

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:02.370
- [Narrator] East Coast
services start at 4:45 a.m.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:05.190
catering for sleep-staved commuters.

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:08.340
16 members of staff rose well before dawn

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.493
to get this Newcastle to
London train up and running.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:14.913
- Coming alive now, I'm coming alive.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:17.880
- [Narrator] Despite
the cosy cooking area,

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.760
Mark still has five
different breakfast dishes

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:21.960
up his sleeve.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.245
- Karen, I hit the bits on the rostis

00:05:24.245 --> 00:05:26.123
so I'll just smooth the rosti down.

00:05:26.123 --> 00:05:28.740
(laughs) Do you know what I mean?

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.210
- [Narrator] Conditions may be cramped

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:33.243
but Mark wouldn't want
to chef anywhere else.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:37.050
- You've got your bonus
that this is a fantastic job

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.450
and you've got your job
security because you know,

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:42.030
you refer to the railway
as a job for life as well

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:43.560
because you just feel very secure

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:45.072
in your job on the railway.

00:05:45.072 --> 00:05:48.239
(upbeat rock music)

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.213
Whoo, it's smoky!

00:05:53.611 --> 00:05:54.444
- [Sue] That was my toast.

00:05:54.444 --> 00:05:57.180
- This is typical-
- This is my toast.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:59.040
- This is Sue toast.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.000
It's lovely as you can see.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.953
- I was distracted.

00:06:01.953 --> 00:06:03.900
- [Narrator] When the
heat's on in the kitchen,

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:07.380
it's often Karen' who keeps
her eye on quality control.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:09.803
- You could never, ever
serve anybody that could you?

00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:12.067
(ticket machine clacking)

00:06:12.067 --> 00:06:14.028
(light clicks)
(bell pings)

00:06:14.028 --> 00:06:17.014
(horn toots)
(train rumbling)

00:06:17.014 --> 00:06:19.681
(gentle music)

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.627
- I went to the job centre
and they said to me,

00:06:23.627 --> 00:06:25.980
"We've got two jobs here.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:29.970
Either one be a butcher
or one join the railway."

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:33.750
And I thought well, transport
sounds interesting to me.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:35.520
None of my family or relatives

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:36.450
have ever been on the railway.

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:40.470
I'm the first person in the
family to work on the railway.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:41.870
So I thought I'll go for it.

00:06:42.935 --> 00:06:46.352
(gentle music continues)

00:06:48.712 --> 00:06:52.545
(destination board clacking)

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:01.650
- [Narrator] 47, yes, 47
years after taking that job,

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:05.730
JR is now one of the York
team's longest serving members.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:08.670
He's done almost every job at the station.

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:12.000
But the thing he loves
best is dispatching trains.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.880
And over his 5 decade long career,

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:18.780
JR has sent off over 50,000 services.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:20.190
- [JR] You want to be further along there.

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.900
You want to be further down,

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:24.060
further down.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:26.880
- [Narrator] Today is
a very important day-

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:27.840
- [JR] 08:43.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.510
- [Narrator] Because after
all those years on the job,

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:32.343
JR is retiring.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:35.220
- Hello, Graham.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:36.210
- Is this your last day?
- It is.

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:38.778
I thought you were... Is that for me?

00:07:38.778 --> 00:07:39.862
- [Graham] Yeah.

00:07:39.862 --> 00:07:41.956
- Thank you very much.
- I'll miss ya.

00:07:41.956 --> 00:07:43.686
- Oh, you're a big mate of mine.

00:07:43.686 --> 00:07:47.186
(upbeat harmonica music)

00:07:49.017 --> 00:07:50.880
- [Narrator] And one of York's regulars,

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:52.140
train-spotter Graham,

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:55.800
is determined to mark
this momentous occasion.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:58.140
- Oh my god.
(paper tearing)

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.590
It's like Christmas all over.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:02.160
Oh, This looks interesting.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:03.170
Let's see what it is.

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:05.070
A decanter, oh that's nice.

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:06.851
Thank you very much, Graham.

00:08:06.851 --> 00:08:08.760
- Remember to fill it
with Scottish whisky.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:10.710
- I will do now.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.060
That's me card.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:13.500
Beautiful.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:14.670
Beautiful.

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:16.027
I'll treasure that.

00:08:16.027 --> 00:08:20.100
"Good luck, Goodbye from
Graham, train-spotter."

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:22.800
Thank you very much. I appreciate that.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.450
- Yeah, I will miss him but
life goes on, doesn't it?

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:28.500
- [Narrator] For JR, it's going to be

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:31.495
a long and emotional final day.

00:08:31.495 --> 00:08:34.860
(upbeat music)
(train tracks clacking)

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:38.553
It's hen and stag-tastic
at Newcastle station.

00:08:40.319 --> 00:08:41.940
(stag group laughing)

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:43.980
Until disaster strikes.

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:46.470
- We reckon there's a total loss of power.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:48.747
- Never happens at the quietest of times.

00:08:48.747 --> 00:08:51.760
(whistle blows)

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:56.760
(train tracks clacking)
(upbeat banjo music)

00:08:57.869 --> 00:08:59.550
(horn blaring)

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.440
- [Narrator] Spanning
the length of Britain,

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.330
the East Coast line clocks up

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:06.720
19 million passenger journeys a year.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.490
It's staff have seen it all.

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:11.520
- I had a lady come into the station

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:14.940
and I could see she was like
holding her face like this.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.077
And her husband ran over and he went,

00:09:17.077 --> 00:09:18.570
"Excuse me! Excuse me!"

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:20.280
I went, "What's the
problem? Can I help you?"

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:24.600
He went, "Me wife's glass
eye has popped out."

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:26.370
He went, "Its on the track!"

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:28.290
What had happened was her son was going

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:31.800
away in the army or something
and she'd cried that much

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:33.480
that the eye had popped out.

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:36.237
We took it back up and I
actually helped the lady wash it

00:09:36.237 --> 00:09:37.650
and she put it back in.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:40.320
And I never laughed all the way through

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:42.397
but it wasn't until
later on Glen said to me,

00:09:42.397 --> 00:09:45.210
"You kept your face straight
all the way through that."

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:47.160
He said, "You even washed her glass eye."

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:48.900
He went, "If that's not
going the extra mile,

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:50.692
I don't know what is."

00:09:50.692 --> 00:09:52.020
(whimsical music)

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.090
- [Narrator] Jumping
eyeballs are not the only

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:58.410
bizarre items the East Coast
staff have to contended with.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:02.220
- We've had wooden legs, all
sorts in our lost property.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:04.530
Blow up dolls, everything.

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:07.350
- [Narrator] At York station,
Julie's been directing trains

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:11.370
and playing mum to
passengers for 14 years.

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:13.074
- What you done, lost your bag?

00:10:13.074 --> 00:10:14.220
- Yeah, I've lost all my bags.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.581
- Where's your case?
- Don't need a case.

00:10:16.581 --> 00:10:17.977
- Oh, you're just going for a day?

00:10:17.977 --> 00:10:19.830
- I'm goin' to the Shard tower.

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:23.520
- Oh look, your coat's
all undone, all not right.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.883
- Well...
- There you go.

00:10:25.883 --> 00:10:27.296
- [Passenger] I need an emergency toilet.

00:10:27.296 --> 00:10:29.727
But it's at the top.
- Oh.

00:10:29.727 --> 00:10:34.727
(whimsical music)
(door thuds)

00:10:36.906 --> 00:10:39.656
(doors thudding)

00:10:43.318 --> 00:10:46.110
(whistle blows)

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:48.750
- [Narrator] Julie's
duties are wide and varied,

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:51.900
and she's used to dealing
with the unexpected.

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:54.960
- From leaving their
children on the platform,

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:58.653
people collapsing, injuries, everything.

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:01.490
I just love that every day is different.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:05.899
You get disruption and it's just lovely.

00:11:05.899 --> 00:11:09.149
(upbeat country music)

00:11:10.016 --> 00:11:15.016
(train rumbling)
(horn blaring)

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:18.510
- [Narrator] Today it's not so happy Abbey

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:20.884
who's caused the disruption.

00:11:20.884 --> 00:11:22.605
- Just letting you know
I'm gonna be a bit late

00:11:22.605 --> 00:11:23.438
'cause I've been an idiot

00:11:23.438 --> 00:11:25.323
and dropped my bags down the train line.

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:28.220
All right I'll see you when I can.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:31.850
- Did it just slip out of your hand?

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:33.760
- It was on here and I lifted it off.

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:36.540
I was an idiot and had
my bag on my suitcase

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:38.400
and lifted it up to get into the carriage

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:40.350
and it just fell down.

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:41.183
Nightmare.

00:11:41.183 --> 00:11:43.260
- [Narrator] It may be
just a little handbag

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:47.463
but any item on the track
signals major disruption.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:50.010
- Hello its Julie Garnet.

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:53.430
Stop the services coming
through platform three

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:55.233
while I'm on the track.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:57.360
Yes please.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:11:58.383
Thank you. Bye.

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:01.500
I've got my high-visible vest on

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:03.870
and I've got my boots on, trousers.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:05.870
I don't want to go down with a skirt on,

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:08.392
show all my legs.

00:12:08.392 --> 00:12:11.725
(upbeat country music)

00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:17.040
- I'm a hairdresser so all of
my equipment's all in my bag.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:18.030
I missed my train now.

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:20.940
I'm on my way to do a
photo shoot in Sheffield.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:21.930
I'm such an idiot.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:23.999
I feel really bad. (laughs)

00:12:23.999 --> 00:12:28.082
(upbeat country music continues)

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:32.910
- [Narrator] With an entire
track out of service,

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:34.743
Julie leaps into action.

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:39.173
- There you go.

00:12:40.145 --> 00:12:41.745
- Thank you so much.
- Alright.

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.490
- [Narrator] You're welcome Abbey.

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:46.663
That's all part of the service.

00:12:51.417 --> 00:12:55.111
- All done and the
lady's got her bag back.

00:12:55.111 --> 00:12:56.602
(key clunks)

00:12:56.602 --> 00:12:57.560
(ticket punch whirs)
(bell pings)

00:12:57.560 --> 00:12:59.538
(train rumbling)

00:12:59.538 --> 00:13:02.205
(vibrant music)

00:13:04.056 --> 00:13:07.889
(destination board clacking)

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:12.888
- [Narrator] Newcastle: a
mecca for stag and hen parties.

00:13:12.888 --> 00:13:16.388
(hen shouts indistinctly)

00:13:18.684 --> 00:13:20.835
(hens cheering)

00:13:20.835 --> 00:13:22.830
It's the last Friday of the month,

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:23.910
payday.

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:26.730
And hundreds of revellers
are arriving at the station

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:30.570
for the start of a big night out.

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:31.403
- Here we go!

00:13:32.836 --> 00:13:35.220
- [Narrator] On the
platform tonight is Daz,

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:38.250
a man who's come across
so many crazy costumes,

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:40.290
he could open a fancy dress shop.

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:42.590
- It was last year or the year before,

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:45.240
there was two tiger suits in lost property

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:47.970
and they'd been left by
obviously some stag party.

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:49.770
So me and Mark put these tiger suits on

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:50.603
and ran around the station.

00:13:50.603 --> 00:13:52.530
It was just absolutely funny as owt.

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:54.258
Everybody was cracking up laughing.

00:13:54.258 --> 00:13:55.091
It was me and Collier

00:13:55.091 --> 00:13:56.310
just running around in these tiger suits.

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:57.677
It was a right laugh.

00:13:57.677 --> 00:14:02.677
(whistle blows)
(upbeat rock music)

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:03.900
- [Narrator] From early afternoon

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:06.720
there are hens aplenty
flouncing on and off trains.

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:08.400
- Are ya gettin' married, mate?

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:11.220
- [Narrator] And ever alert
to the call to action,

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:12.273
Banana Man,

00:14:13.187 --> 00:14:14.805
(stags cackling)

00:14:14.805 --> 00:14:17.790
who is less alert than
a superhero should be.

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:19.290
- Always wear clean pants, kids,

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:20.573
in case you get hit by a car.

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:23.916
- I was going to say should we let you go,

00:14:23.916 --> 00:14:27.162
But he wasn't packing much there. (laughs)

00:14:27.162 --> 00:14:30.104
(whimsical electro reggae music)

00:14:30.104 --> 00:14:31.130
- [Narrator] Daz has been working

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:33.630
at Newcastle station for 12 years,

00:14:33.630 --> 00:14:37.200
and payday Friday shifts
are always the liveliest.

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:39.390
- The problems we get
with stag and hen parties,

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:41.943
we get a lot of delay of
trains because what they do is,

00:14:41.943 --> 00:14:44.070
they obviously just sit
until the very last moment

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:45.210
and they hear the
announcements on the train

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:46.800
but they're not really paying attention.

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:47.760
Then when the train stops,

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:48.720
they're like looking out the window

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.741
and they can see the
Newcastle sign and it's like,

00:14:50.741 --> 00:14:52.343
"We're here."

00:14:52.343 --> 00:14:53.996
Alright girls I'll have to rush you.

00:14:53.996 --> 00:14:57.413
(whimsical reggae music)

00:14:58.943 --> 00:15:01.032
I've had incidents where people
have put their windows down

00:15:01.032 --> 00:15:01.865
and they've actually physically

00:15:01.865 --> 00:15:03.360
dived out onto the platform

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.340
'cause obviously they've
realised that it's their station.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:06.673
- That's the bride to be.

00:15:08.627 --> 00:15:09.630
- [Daz] What the hell you got in that?

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:10.980
- [Narrator] Daz has his hands full

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:13.477
with a station full of party animals.

00:15:13.477 --> 00:15:15.270
- I was gonna say. (laughs)

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:16.920
- [Narrator] But his shift is about to get

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:18.243
a whole lot harder.

00:15:22.546 --> 00:15:26.222
(distorted radio message)

00:15:26.222 --> 00:15:29.810
(destination board clacking)
(tense music)

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:32.940
- [Narrator] 90 miles south
at East Coast control centre,

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:34.233
there's high drama.

00:15:35.190 --> 00:15:37.980
A lightening bolt has
hit some of the signals,

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:40.890
and this could mean chaos for the network,

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:42.540
and for Daz.

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:44.519
- East Coast Control, Robin speaking.

00:15:44.519 --> 00:15:45.720
(phone ringing)

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:49.140
Basically at about 3 o'clock
there was a thunderstorm

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:52.560
south of Berwick that's knocked
out some of the signalling.

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:55.275
What we've had to do is
cancel trains at Edinburgh,

00:15:55.275 --> 00:15:57.300
and cancel trains at Newcastle,

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:00.527
to stop sending trains
towards the affected area.

00:16:00.527 --> 00:16:03.454
(upbeat electro music)

00:16:03.454 --> 00:16:04.760
- We reckon there's a total loss of power.

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:06.960
So the next two trains
I think will make it in

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:09.060
and then we might have nothing for awhile.

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:12.270
- [Narrator] Delays are
bad at the best of times,

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:15.780
but on a busy Friday, they're disastrous.

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:19.737
17 trains an hour, each
carrying around 400 passengers,

00:16:19.737 --> 00:16:22.533
are scheduled to pass
through Newcastle today.

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:25.140
With the service stalled,

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:28.710
disgruntled passengers will
not only get stuck on board,

00:16:28.710 --> 00:16:30.480
thousands could also start to pile up

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.253
on the station platforms.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:36.710
- And you're talking tens
of thousands of passengers

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:39.240
packed into the doorways as well.

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:40.073
And obviously it's rammed;

00:16:40.073 --> 00:16:42.350
we've just got a sea of
passengers everywhere.

00:16:46.950 --> 00:16:50.010
- A lot of people going home
for the weekend et cetera,

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:52.020
going away for the weekend.

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:55.280
You know, Sod's law, but
that's the way it is.

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:58.350
It never happens at the quietest of times.

00:16:58.350 --> 00:17:00.660
- [Narrator] Looks like
Daz's Payday Friday

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:03.296
could become the shift from hell!

00:17:03.296 --> 00:17:04.368
(tone beeps)
(punch clicks)

00:17:04.368 --> 00:17:05.251
(whistle blows)

00:17:05.251 --> 00:17:08.255
(relaxed blues music)

00:17:08.255 --> 00:17:11.250
In York, veteran of the railways, JR,

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:14.163
has seen his fair share
of crisis management.

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:18.600
But today his shift is not
about controlling chaos,

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:20.763
it's about saying goodbye.

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:27.540
- There's three teams on York station,

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:29.700
and to me it's like my family.

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:31.590
So I meet 'em regular,

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:33.450
go for a drink or see 'em somewhere,

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:36.090
go round to see 'em at their
house, have a drink with them.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:39.210
Oh yeah they'll all keep friendly,

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:42.090
yeah always be there, always be my mates.

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:46.260
- All the best to you
darling. You take care.

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:49.500
- When I left me house this
morning I thought it's weird.

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:52.020
But now I come back here
at 2 o'clock or whatever

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:53.577
I've finished.
- That's it.

00:17:53.577 --> 00:17:56.880
- And this gentleman here
is always giving me mints.

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:00.120
Aren't you? He looks after
me on the sweet department.

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:00.953
See you later.

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:03.750
- [Narrator] There's one person who means

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:05.700
more to him than all the rest:

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:10.394
his teammate of 17 years
and current boss, Paola.

00:18:10.394 --> 00:18:13.561
(warm, gentle music)

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:22.050
- JR is loved honestly by everybody here.

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:23.550
He's just always been the same.

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:26.193
He comes into work positive, upbeat,

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:29.160
always the same.

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:31.113
Never, never sad.

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:34.310
- Oh what's that?

00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:35.897
Forever Friends.

00:18:35.897 --> 00:18:37.320
And we will be.

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:40.770
We will be always friends for
the rest of our lives, Paola.

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:42.300
- Okay.
- Alright.

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:43.320
- Enjoy today.

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:44.730
- I know what'll happen now I'm going,

00:18:44.730 --> 00:18:45.721
change the locks.

00:18:45.721 --> 00:18:46.993
(laughs) I'm only joking.

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:51.510
"Hey, Gaggy, keep him out."

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:52.343
They've changed all t'locks.

00:18:52.343 --> 00:18:55.330
"He's gone. Change 'em." (laughs)

00:18:58.443 --> 00:18:59.276
That's it.

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:02.910
- We do actually change the codes

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:06.355
on the door when people leave. (chuckles)

00:19:06.355 --> 00:19:11.355
But that's just what we do as
part of process and security.

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:14.523
But he'll never be,
he'll never be shut out.

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:18.210
- [Narrator] As one of
Paola's team takes his leave,

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:20.640
another new recruit's life on the railway

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:21.873
is just beginning.

00:19:22.770 --> 00:19:25.050
- I was in my previous
role as a fish monger

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:27.570
and one of my customers was
Paola, the station manager now.

00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:28.950
And liked what she saw in me,

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:29.970
thought I'd be good at the job.

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:32.070
I got, you could say poached,

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:33.660
it's a massive change for me.

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:36.387
But it's the best decision
I ever made to be honest.

00:19:36.387 --> 00:19:38.130
(inquisitive music)

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:39.810
- [Narrator] Paola might have poached Liam

00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:42.600
away from the salmon, but
he's got a lot to prove

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:45.873
before he's ready to
dispatch trains like JR.

00:19:48.413 --> 00:19:52.443
At the moment his main
task is station security.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:56.700
- Every hour I have to do
a full lap of the station.

00:19:56.700 --> 00:19:58.140
So I'm looking for anything suspicious,

00:19:58.140 --> 00:19:59.820
any hidden packages,

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:01.920
all in the platforms, the travel centre,

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:04.050
the car parks, the bike racks.

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:06.210
I've got to check all that within one hour

00:20:06.210 --> 00:20:08.756
and I've got to do that
every hour for my full shift.

00:20:08.756 --> 00:20:11.923
(funky upbeat music)

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:14.700
- [Narrator] Railway rookie Liam

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:17.223
has to be extra-specially vigilant.

00:20:17.223 --> 00:20:18.056
(toilet flushing)

00:20:18.056 --> 00:20:19.380
- Apparently there's
some suspicious youths

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:23.250
just up round the cycle racks
a few minutes ago, drinking.

00:20:23.250 --> 00:20:25.740
I don't think they were
probably old enough to drink,

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:27.713
but the police are on their way anyways.

00:20:28.650 --> 00:20:31.143
I'm pretty sure they've cleared off.

00:20:31.143 --> 00:20:34.130
But I have to go down this
platform anyway, so...

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:37.200
The police have gone around
the bike racks as well

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:38.370
so it's just to check that those youths

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:41.253
have definitely cleared off,
which it appears they have.

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:45.090
At random intervals also,
the department of transport,

00:20:45.090 --> 00:20:47.700
they will come and test us.

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:51.180
They can and quite often
do plant something.

00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:54.090
So last time it was a
small black hold-all.

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:56.310
But they give us two
hours really to find it.

00:20:56.310 --> 00:20:59.459
So they're the hide and
seek experts really.

00:20:59.459 --> 00:21:02.010
- [Producer] Did you
find it on that occasion?

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:04.675
- The last time, no I didn't.

00:21:04.675 --> 00:21:06.210
(funky upbeat music)

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:08.130
So I really feel the pressure
now to make sure next time

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:09.637
they come that I've got find it.

00:21:09.637 --> 00:21:11.100
So everyone will think, "Yeah,
Liam's great at his job,

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:12.060
Liam's gonna find it.

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:14.550
If Liam's on duty we know we're safe."

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:15.568
Yeah, no worries, Paola.

00:21:15.568 --> 00:21:17.490
I'm on platform seven but
I'll make my way over.

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:18.323
Over.

00:21:20.287 --> 00:21:21.120
(radio crackles)

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:24.302
- [Narrator] Liam's been called
to check a security alert.

00:21:24.302 --> 00:21:25.977
(indistinct radio alert)

00:21:25.977 --> 00:21:27.877
- Just had a message of
some unattended luggage

00:21:27.877 --> 00:21:29.010
at the front of the station.

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:32.160
So this is when I come in use for them.

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:34.933
So I have to have a look at that.

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:38.280
- [Narrator] The unattended
bag could represent

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:40.380
a major security threat.

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:42.900
Liam is straight on the case.

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:43.900
- Let's have a look.

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:49.263
Yeah as predicted, pretty much nothing.

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:52.643
Nothing.

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:56.150
No, I think we're all clear.

00:21:57.510 --> 00:21:59.271
Safe to fight another day.

00:21:59.271 --> 00:22:01.125
(upbeat music)
(train whooshing)

00:22:01.125 --> 00:22:02.550
(destination board clacking)

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:05.520
- [Narrator] Problems
pile up on payday Friday.

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:07.590
- And the more build up
of passengers there is,

00:22:07.590 --> 00:22:09.117
then it becomes really dangerous.

00:22:09.117 --> 00:22:12.491
- [Narrator] And while the hens
make the best of a bad day-

00:22:12.491 --> 00:22:13.980
(hens cheering)

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:16.140
it could be game over for the stags.

00:22:16.140 --> 00:22:19.440
- The only person who
caught the last train north

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:20.967
was the stag.

00:22:20.967 --> 00:22:22.371
(jaunty banjo music)

00:22:22.371 --> 00:22:26.121
(destination board clacking)

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:32.970
- [Narrator] It's Payday
Party Friday in Newcastle

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:36.000
and Information Controller
Daz is in the middle

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:39.240
of one of the most stressful
shifts of his year.

00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:41.520
Everybody wants to start their weekend

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:44.370
but thousands of passengers
are going nowhere fast.

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:46.950
- All I'm gonna do is
ask you to wait awhile.

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:49.950
There's nothing moving at the
moment north of Newcastle.

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:51.870
So if you have a seat in the
waiting room on the platforms.

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:53.490
As soon as we know something,
we're gonna come round

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:55.500
but listen to our
announcements as well, yeah.

00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:56.610
Have a seat in the waiting room

00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:57.757
or have a seat on the platform.

00:22:57.757 --> 00:22:59.040
As soon as we know we'll let yous know.

00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:00.665
Listen for announcements,
though, madam, okay?

00:23:00.665 --> 00:23:02.095
- [Passenger] Alright, thanks very much.

00:23:02.095 --> 00:23:07.095
(upbeat rock music)
(destination board clacking)

00:23:07.250 --> 00:23:09.990
- [Narrator] 90 miles south
in the central control room,

00:23:09.990 --> 00:23:12.150
it's controlled chaos because of an

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:15.480
unexpected lightning strike.
- 45 minutes.

00:23:15.480 --> 00:23:18.510
Who am I to stop thunder and lightning?

00:23:18.510 --> 00:23:21.000
I'm afraid, you know, that does happen.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:23.190
The systems short out.

00:23:23.190 --> 00:23:25.470
We don't tender with a situation unsafe

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:29.040
and we don't tender with a
train heading into an area

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:31.155
where there is no signalling at all.

00:23:31.155 --> 00:23:33.480
(jaunty music)
(train rattling)

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:36.120
- [Narrator] Four trains
heading north from Kings Cross,

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:38.730
and three trains heading
south from Scotland

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:41.520
have all been stopped in their tracks.

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:43.290
- There's a waiting room there
if you want to keep warm.

00:23:43.290 --> 00:23:45.536
As soon as we know we'll let you know.

00:23:45.536 --> 00:23:47.070
Cheers. Thank you.

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:49.110
The hardest thing is
obviously the passengers

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:51.143
all want to know what's going on.

00:23:51.143 --> 00:23:52.166
I don't know anything.
(radio chatter crackles)

00:23:52.166 --> 00:23:53.130
I'm trying to listen to that

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:54.570
and keep the trains
going at the same time,

00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:55.620
as well as everybody talking.

00:23:55.620 --> 00:23:57.243
So it makes it quite hard.

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:01.830
- [Narrator] Over 3,000
passengers are stuck on trains,

00:24:01.830 --> 00:24:03.960
and the number waiting
at Newcastle station

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:05.880
is about to hit a thousand.

00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:08.613
It's up to Daz to keep calm and carry on.

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:12.240
- I do believe there's been buses ordered

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:14.059
but we'll obviously have to
wait and see what happens

00:24:14.059 --> 00:24:15.017
in the next half an hour.

00:24:15.017 --> 00:24:17.520
- It's a good night out in
Newcastle anyway. (laughs)

00:24:17.520 --> 00:24:19.680
- Party city, yeah. Okay.

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:22.230
- [Narrator] Central
control has to take action

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:24.480
before the situation gets worse.

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:25.620
- Just bear with me.

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:29.100
Nickola, number 1-9 is
nearly in Newcastle.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:31.410
So that could form Echo 2-1.

00:24:31.410 --> 00:24:33.330
- [Narrator] The decision
is taken to terminate

00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:35.850
all northbound trains at Newcastle

00:24:35.850 --> 00:24:37.710
and send them back down south,

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:40.230
keeping the service
running between Newcastle

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:42.270
and London at least.

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:44.580
- Right, this is the 15:58 to London.

00:24:44.580 --> 00:24:45.510
You can get on now.

00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:48.393
Anybody travelling on the 15:58.

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:51.180
- [Narrator] So for Daz, it's all change

00:24:51.180 --> 00:24:54.183
as his northbound trains
have just become southbound.

00:24:55.159 --> 00:24:58.492
(up-tempo dance music)

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:04.500
- Right let's get it
away. Let's get it away.

00:25:08.820 --> 00:25:09.653
All right bud?

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:12.543
No crew? No buffet crew?

00:25:13.530 --> 00:25:14.730
I'm not worried about the buffet crew

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:16.320
but we've got a guard and a driver.

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:17.252
That'll do yeah.

00:25:17.252 --> 00:25:18.598
Cheers though. Thank you.

00:25:18.598 --> 00:25:23.598
(upbeat rock music)
(whistle blows)

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:26.940
- [Narrator] With the Southbound
passengers out of his hair,

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:29.880
Daz still has to deal with
the northbound passengers

00:25:29.880 --> 00:25:32.220
stranded on the other platforms.

00:25:32.220 --> 00:25:34.230
Their trains are still not running,

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:37.620
but although many are all
dressed up with nowhere to go,

00:25:37.620 --> 00:25:40.266
some have come prepared.

00:25:40.266 --> 00:25:43.933
(hens singing and laughing)

00:25:48.399 --> 00:25:52.480
- We've got some gin and tins
and we're just having a party.

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:54.663
- Train's delayed but we're
making the most of it.

00:25:54.663 --> 00:25:57.128
- We're having the hen
party on the station.

00:25:57.128 --> 00:25:59.434
- -We're having a platform party.

00:25:59.434 --> 00:26:02.420
My little sister is getting
married so every guy around

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:03.870
is gonna be crying their eyes out

00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:06.098
because this beautiful lady is taken.

00:26:06.098 --> 00:26:06.972
Well done, Nicki.

00:26:06.972 --> 00:26:09.555
(all cheering)

00:26:10.564 --> 00:26:12.200
- [Narrator] Cheers, ladies.

00:26:12.200 --> 00:26:14.613
If only lads were as well organised.

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:18.723
- We're here on a stag do.

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:20.700
22 people,

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:24.330
the only person that
caught the last train north

00:26:24.330 --> 00:26:27.150
was the stag, who is
now at Edinburgh station

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:29.970
on his own in a kilt,
dressed like Mel Gibson.

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:32.073
He looks a state and he's on his own.

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:34.370
We're all a bit sad for him.

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:37.230
The one rule,

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:41.010
the one rule is that no
one leaves the stag alone,

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:44.643
and 21 other people left the stag alone.

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:48.060
We have completely dropped the ball.

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:49.200
- We don't know what to do now.

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:51.270
We don't know how to get to Edinburgh.

00:26:51.270 --> 00:26:53.081
We're completely lost.

00:26:53.081 --> 00:26:54.480
- (chuckles) You've got me.

00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:56.036
- Rebecca here doesn't know what to do.

00:26:56.036 --> 00:26:58.091
- We have Rebecca. We met Rebecca.

00:26:58.091 --> 00:27:03.091
(languid acoustic music)
(bustle of passengers)

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:09.210
- [Narrator] So far Daz has
managed to keep a cool head.

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:11.250
But as the disruption continues,

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:12.990
he starts to feel the pressure.

00:27:12.990 --> 00:27:14.460
- The more trains that
come in and terminate,

00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:16.320
the more passengers
we're going to have here.

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:17.700
As you can see from
them, there's quite a lot

00:27:17.700 --> 00:27:19.470
of people close to the
edges and that, you know.

00:27:19.470 --> 00:27:21.570
And the more buildup
of passengers there is,

00:27:21.570 --> 00:27:22.620
then it becomes really dangerous

00:27:22.620 --> 00:27:24.510
and it's obviously a concern, you know.

00:27:24.510 --> 00:27:26.370
But being a Friday, it couldn't get worse.

00:27:26.370 --> 00:27:28.020
It can't get worse than a Friday.

00:27:29.344 --> 00:27:30.256
(socket wrench clicks)
(printer whirs)

00:27:30.256 --> 00:27:31.157
(keyring jingles)

00:27:31.157 --> 00:27:35.100
(upbeat music)
(train clacking)

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:38.730
- [Narrator] 270 miles away,
down south at King's Cross,

00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:41.310
self-confessed trolley-dolly Jack

00:27:41.310 --> 00:27:43.844
is preparing for show time!

00:27:43.844 --> 00:27:47.344
(confident upbeat music)

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:55.860
Jack used to fancy treading the boards,

00:27:55.860 --> 00:27:58.380
but since starting his job on the railway,

00:27:58.380 --> 00:28:01.950
he's realised there's no
more captive an audience

00:28:01.950 --> 00:28:03.810
than one trapped on a train
(horn blaring)

00:28:03.810 --> 00:28:05.250
- If I'm there and I'm having fun

00:28:05.250 --> 00:28:06.750
and I'm talking to the customers

00:28:06.750 --> 00:28:07.797
and I'm being a bit show-bizzy

00:28:07.797 --> 00:28:09.900
and having a bit of fun with them,

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:12.240
then that makes my day go quicker.

00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:13.770
If they're then enjoying their journey

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:15.390
because I'm being a bit silly,

00:28:15.390 --> 00:28:17.010
then that makes their
journey go a bit quicker.

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:18.453
Everyone has a nicer day.

00:28:19.590 --> 00:28:21.000
Good evening ladies and gentlemen.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:22.110
My name is Jack Allen,

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:23.880
catering crew leader for your journey.

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:26.760
I did performing arts
when I was in college

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:27.593
as an A-level.

00:28:27.593 --> 00:28:30.930
So I mean maybe, it
probably stems from that.

00:28:30.930 --> 00:28:32.370
For those passengers in standard class

00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:33.780
who do wish to remain seated,

00:28:33.780 --> 00:28:35.940
the trolley service is making
its way through the train.

00:28:35.940 --> 00:28:38.970
Yeah that's my stage. (laughs)

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:41.374
Any teas, coffees, cold drinks?

00:28:41.374 --> 00:28:44.453
(upbeat rock music)
♪ Na, na-na-na ♪

00:28:45.443 --> 00:28:47.640
♪ Na-na, na-na-na ♪

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:49.560
- Just make sure that's
actually the right items;

00:28:49.560 --> 00:28:52.573
that I haven't charged you
for something you've not got.

00:28:52.573 --> 00:28:53.430
That okay? No worries.

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:54.510
I'll chuck that way for you if you want.

00:28:54.510 --> 00:28:55.590
It's not part of the receipt.

00:28:55.590 --> 00:28:58.680
I just like going through
chatting rubbish to people.

00:28:58.680 --> 00:28:59.980
I do it to confuse people.

00:29:00.886 --> 00:29:03.210
(Jack chuckling)

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:05.730
I know what I'm doing. Professional, me.

00:29:05.730 --> 00:29:08.010
Any refreshments at all?

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:09.180
Oops, sorry.

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:10.410
I'm throwing milk at people now.

00:29:10.410 --> 00:29:12.750
It's alright, I won't
drop that one on you.

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:14.100
That'd be really mean of me.

00:29:14.100 --> 00:29:16.740
It's kind of like manoeuvring
a full shopping cart down,

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:18.600
well, down a moving train.

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:19.650
Sorry, is that your bag there sir?

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:21.930
- Yeah. Sorry.
- Sorry, that's alright.

00:29:21.930 --> 00:29:24.150
This is quite heavy, I don't
want to damage anything.

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:25.110
Until you get the hang of it,

00:29:25.110 --> 00:29:27.360
you're constantly crashing
into people's seats

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:29.400
and nearly running people over,

00:29:29.400 --> 00:29:33.286
which most people tend
not to be too happy about.

00:29:33.286 --> 00:29:35.869
(upbeat music)

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:42.420
- [Narrator] And while
mastering his trolley act,

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:44.820
Jack's bumped into all sorts.

00:29:44.820 --> 00:29:47.700
- We actually had Prince Philip travelling

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:50.820
with sort of six or seven of
his security detail. (laughs)

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:52.800
who were mildly terrifying.

00:29:52.800 --> 00:29:55.350
I was very worried that I
would get something wrong.

00:29:55.350 --> 00:29:57.060
I was worried the train would jolt

00:29:57.060 --> 00:29:58.680
and I would get coffee everywhere.

00:29:58.680 --> 00:30:00.513
Luckily that didn't happen.

00:30:01.620 --> 00:30:04.470
I did that really awkward
and annoying thing,

00:30:04.470 --> 00:30:07.687
that when you're talking to
someone, your voice breaks.

00:30:07.687 --> 00:30:10.500
So, "Can I get you any
(squeaks) coffee sir?"

00:30:10.500 --> 00:30:11.392
Oh no.

00:30:11.392 --> 00:30:14.760
(upbeat music resumes)

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:16.440
- [Narrator] Eagle eyed staff might even

00:30:16.440 --> 00:30:20.220
get to spot their favourite
celeb somewhere along the line

00:30:20.220 --> 00:30:23.610
- Hugh Grant was trying
to get into the toilet

00:30:23.610 --> 00:30:25.830
which was closed for painting.

00:30:25.830 --> 00:30:28.980
So I took my chance, ran up,

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:31.440
asked him if he'd like to use the toilet

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:33.420
and took him upstairs to the office.

00:30:33.420 --> 00:30:35.520
I found myself in the
staff kitchen with him.

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:37.260
He asked me where the toilet was

00:30:37.260 --> 00:30:38.220
and when I went to show him,

00:30:38.220 --> 00:30:39.330
nothing came out of my mouth

00:30:39.330 --> 00:30:41.643
because I was so sort of in awe.

00:30:42.690 --> 00:30:45.930
We have a sign on the back
of the toilet door now:

00:30:45.930 --> 00:30:48.022
Hugh Grant sat here.

00:30:48.022 --> 00:30:50.160
(bright reggae music)

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:51.870
- [Narrator] It's not just Hollywood stars

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:54.693
and royalty who frequent
the East Coast line.

00:30:55.680 --> 00:30:57.960
- We're on the 11:00 from
Kings Cross to Edinburgh

00:30:57.960 --> 00:31:00.183
and Sting's up there.

00:31:03.960 --> 00:31:06.750
I think I've got a bit of
an eye for it if I'm honest.

00:31:06.750 --> 00:31:09.210
Its not that I'm on the look
out but I usually do pick up

00:31:09.210 --> 00:31:10.982
pretty quickly if I see someone famous.

00:31:10.982 --> 00:31:14.940
(bright reggae music)
(train rumbling)

00:31:14.940 --> 00:31:16.470
- Missed him unfortunately
but Paul McCartney

00:31:16.470 --> 00:31:18.360
was on station a few weeks ago.

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:20.820
- Bobby Robson, I might
add he's fantastic.

00:31:20.820 --> 00:31:23.280
He was in when, obviously
before he passed away.

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:25.440
- Jeremy Paxman, I used to
work at Berwick for a while.

00:31:25.440 --> 00:31:27.019
He was really nice,

00:31:27.019 --> 00:31:28.050
a big bloke, like, a really tall bloke.

00:31:28.050 --> 00:31:29.580
You know when you get to see
people in like real life,

00:31:29.580 --> 00:31:31.620
you don't realise how
some of them are small

00:31:31.620 --> 00:31:33.087
or some of them are really big, you know.

00:31:33.087 --> 00:31:36.030
- [Narrator] And some of
them are larger than life.

00:31:36.030 --> 00:31:38.790
- If anybody wants the
paper I'll leave it here.

00:31:38.790 --> 00:31:40.080
Recycled.

00:31:40.080 --> 00:31:42.810
Thank you for the lovely journey again.

00:31:42.810 --> 00:31:44.400
- [Narrator] Bye-di-bye, Sue.

00:31:44.400 --> 00:31:46.533
- Next leg of the journey: Skegness!

00:31:47.670 --> 00:31:49.321
Bye.

00:31:49.321 --> 00:31:50.161
(lock clicks)
(ticket scrapes)

00:31:50.161 --> 00:31:51.186
(glass clinks)

00:31:51.186 --> 00:31:53.947
(horn blaring)

00:31:53.947 --> 00:31:57.197
(lively fiddle music)

00:31:58.050 --> 00:32:01.650
- [Narrator] Keeping cool with
famous faces is one thing,

00:32:01.650 --> 00:32:05.010
but one VIP who gets
everybody hot under the collar

00:32:05.010 --> 00:32:06.900
is a customer so important

00:32:06.900 --> 00:32:09.753
they have to go completely incognito.

00:32:13.050 --> 00:32:16.380
She, who shall not be named or even seen,

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:19.350
is what's known as a - [Mystery Shopper]

00:32:19.350 --> 00:32:22.983
mysterious by name and blurry by nature.

00:32:23.910 --> 00:32:25.797
- Can I just say something to you?

00:32:25.797 --> 00:32:27.300
I've no idea...
- Today it's Bianca,

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:29.790
Jack and Beth's turn to be spied on.

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:31.410
But they don't even know it.

00:32:31.410 --> 00:32:33.840
- I'm definitely a people
person as you can probably tell.

00:32:33.840 --> 00:32:37.650
I can't stop talking so
it's the perfect job for me.

00:32:37.650 --> 00:32:39.960
I just enjoy it 'cause
every day is different.

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:42.240
You never meet the same sort of people.

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:43.530
- Bianca and I get on well.

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:46.713
She talks an awful lot
but then that's Bianca.

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:49.650
- [Narrator] They think they could spot

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:51.843
an undercover agent a mile off.

00:32:52.935 --> 00:32:54.418
- You can tell. You can tell.

00:32:54.418 --> 00:32:56.850
- Some of them can be sort of obvious.

00:32:56.850 --> 00:32:58.500
They'll be writing things down,

00:32:58.500 --> 00:32:59.940
they'll be sitting in certain carriages.

00:32:59.940 --> 00:33:01.830
- [Beth] And they'll
ask for specialty teas.

00:33:01.830 --> 00:33:03.720
- Yeah, the ones that go the extra mile

00:33:03.720 --> 00:33:05.105
but then ask you to sort of,

00:33:05.105 --> 00:33:06.810
"Oh can I have a bottle of water?"

00:33:06.810 --> 00:33:08.124
And you say, "Yeah no problem;

00:33:08.124 --> 00:33:09.150
I'll bring that down for you."

00:33:09.150 --> 00:33:10.260
And as long as you bring it back.

00:33:10.260 --> 00:33:11.853
So they know things like that.

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:14.430
- [Narrator] But today Bianca and Beth's

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:17.470
secret service radar is way off course.

00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:20.947
- Hello. Can I get you anything
to drink or some biscuits?

00:33:20.947 --> 00:33:22.775
- [Mystery Shopper] Yeah.
Have you got any apple juice?

00:33:22.775 --> 00:33:24.692
- Unfortunately I've only
got orange this morning.

00:33:24.692 --> 00:33:26.970
- I'll have orange please.
- Orange, okay?

00:33:26.970 --> 00:33:27.921
Any biscuits for you?

00:33:27.921 --> 00:33:29.871
- No thank you.
- There you go.

00:33:29.871 --> 00:33:33.330
Thank you very much.
- Thank you.

00:33:33.330 --> 00:33:35.100
- We had one guy that was on once.

00:33:35.100 --> 00:33:36.660
We were going to Leeds.

00:33:36.660 --> 00:33:40.140
He came up from London,
got off at Wakefield,

00:33:40.140 --> 00:33:42.090
we came straight back half an hour later

00:33:42.090 --> 00:33:43.383
and he got back on again.

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:45.937
We were sort of all
stood on the train going,

00:33:45.937 --> 00:33:47.280
"I don't think you did much

00:33:47.280 --> 00:33:49.180
while you were in Wakefield, did you?"

00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:53.271
But most of the time you can't
really tell who they are.

00:33:53.271 --> 00:33:56.460
(upbeat music)

00:33:56.460 --> 00:33:59.673
- [Narrator] So, will their
customer service make the grade?

00:34:02.695 --> 00:34:06.278
(quiet indistinct chatter)

00:34:07.629 --> 00:34:11.129
(upbeat music continues)

00:34:13.680 --> 00:34:16.410
Did Bianca and Beth's on-board performance

00:34:16.410 --> 00:34:18.420
impress the mystery shopper?

00:34:18.420 --> 00:34:20.130
- [Mystery Shopper] They
seemed like a very nice team.

00:34:20.130 --> 00:34:21.150
They worked well together.

00:34:21.150 --> 00:34:22.470
They worked hard.

00:34:22.470 --> 00:34:23.607
They were good, they were really good

00:34:23.607 --> 00:34:24.990
and they were cheerful with it

00:34:24.990 --> 00:34:27.090
and they obviously wanted you to enjoy it.

00:34:28.829 --> 00:34:32.940
(fast-paced music)
(destination board clacking)

00:34:32.940 --> 00:34:35.310
- [Narrator] Daz tries
to keep his spirits up.

00:34:35.310 --> 00:34:36.143
- I stink of booze.

00:34:36.143 --> 00:34:39.218
Someone will probably complain
that I've been drinking.

00:34:39.218 --> 00:34:40.320
(whistle blows)

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:43.320
- [Narrator] And JR
faces the final curtain.

00:34:43.320 --> 00:34:46.476
- That's the last train I'll
ever dispatch in my life.

00:34:46.476 --> 00:34:48.059
- Are you alright?
- Yeah.

00:34:48.059 --> 00:34:49.224
- Aw.
(JR chuckles)

00:34:49.224 --> 00:34:52.621
(bright music)

00:34:52.621 --> 00:34:57.250
(destination board clacking)

00:34:57.250 --> 00:35:00.030
- [Narrator] After 47
years of loyal service,

00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:03.240
JR is working out his ever last shift.

00:35:03.240 --> 00:35:05.700
That's got be worth a party!

00:35:05.700 --> 00:35:06.603
- Oh my God.

00:35:07.830 --> 00:35:09.360
Oh that's fantastic.

00:35:09.360 --> 00:35:10.634
Look at this.

00:35:10.634 --> 00:35:11.730
- Your favourites.

00:35:11.730 --> 00:35:13.380
- My favourite is pork pies.

00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:15.527
My god. It's fantastic.

00:35:16.393 --> 00:35:17.906
Whoa! Oh my god.

00:35:17.906 --> 00:35:20.184
Oh, it's fantastic.

00:35:20.184 --> 00:35:21.377
- Party time.

00:35:21.377 --> 00:35:25.922
JR, on behalf of us all,
we wish you the best.

00:35:25.922 --> 00:35:28.672
Have a long and happy retirement.

00:35:29.574 --> 00:35:31.042
- It'll be okay.

00:35:31.042 --> 00:35:32.959
- I'll be alright.

00:35:32.959 --> 00:35:35.292
- Right, all the best, John.

00:35:36.502 --> 00:35:37.813
I'll see you later on.
- Thank you.

00:35:37.813 --> 00:35:39.830
- See you later, darlin'.
- See you later.

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:41.330
- [JR] Oh my word.

00:35:46.157 --> 00:35:47.878
(JR chuckling)

00:35:47.878 --> 00:35:48.750
- Aw.

00:35:48.750 --> 00:35:51.603
- [Narrator] But JR's
final day is far from over.

00:35:52.503 --> 00:35:55.503
(melancholic music)

00:35:56.730 --> 00:35:59.760
He's still got his last
ever train to dispatch.

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:02.800
- Alright?
- I'm feeling all right.

00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:05.550
It's a shame it's gonna be
my last service to dispatch.

00:36:07.764 --> 00:36:09.483
As soon as I go out to the platform,

00:36:09.483 --> 00:36:11.430
it's my last train for the rest of my life

00:36:11.430 --> 00:36:13.713
so it's gonna be emotional for me.

00:36:14.760 --> 00:36:16.780
I'm very upset about it, really.

00:36:18.480 --> 00:36:22.020
- [Narrator] JR dispatched
his first train in 1987

00:36:22.020 --> 00:36:25.203
and he's seen off over
30 a day ever since.

00:36:26.070 --> 00:36:27.870
- That's first stop London, sir.

00:36:27.870 --> 00:36:29.392
Coach where, sir?
- E!

00:36:29.392 --> 00:36:31.320
- E. There it is, just behind you.

00:36:31.320 --> 00:36:32.153
Okay.

00:36:33.121 --> 00:36:35.970
(melancholic music)

00:36:35.970 --> 00:36:37.383
Here we go, me last train.

00:36:38.764 --> 00:36:41.847
(JR blowing whistle)

00:36:49.350 --> 00:36:51.300
Just watch it out, make sure it's okay.

00:36:56.268 --> 00:36:58.177
(horn blaring)

00:36:58.177 --> 00:36:59.010
He's papping his horn.

00:36:59.010 --> 00:37:02.574
He must know it's me
last train. (chuckles)

00:37:02.574 --> 00:37:05.657
(carriages rumbling)

00:37:11.274 --> 00:37:15.191
(melancholic music continues)

00:37:23.676 --> 00:37:24.926
There she goes.

00:37:32.199 --> 00:37:35.127
(horn blaring)

00:37:35.127 --> 00:37:37.510
That's it, aint it, Paola.

00:37:37.510 --> 00:37:39.563
That's my last train I'll
ever dispatch in my life.

00:37:40.560 --> 00:37:41.460
- Are you all right?
- Yeah.

00:37:41.460 --> 00:37:43.830
- Aw.
(JR chuckles)

00:37:43.830 --> 00:37:46.740
- Oh dear me. I'm a bit sad about that.

00:37:46.740 --> 00:37:48.361
Anyway, I'll be all right.

00:37:48.361 --> 00:37:49.194
- [Paola] Come on.

00:37:49.194 --> 00:37:52.620
- [Narrator] JR's 47 years
of service to the line,

00:37:52.620 --> 00:37:55.387
and to Joe Public, is complete.

00:37:55.387 --> 00:37:56.220
(cutlery clanking)

00:37:56.220 --> 00:37:57.053
(radio hisses)
(wrench squeaks)

00:37:57.053 --> 00:37:59.220
(upbeat music)
(destination board clacking)

00:37:59.220 --> 00:38:03.303
But back at Newcastle,
Daz's job is far from over.

00:38:05.340 --> 00:38:08.070
It's payday and hen-and-stag-do Friday

00:38:08.070 --> 00:38:11.430
and after two hours of
delays in both directions,

00:38:11.430 --> 00:38:14.613
the first train from
Edinburgh finally arrives.

00:38:16.125 --> 00:38:18.875
(stags shouting)

00:38:20.670 --> 00:38:22.500
The train needs to be cleaned quickly

00:38:22.500 --> 00:38:26.070
before it's turned around
and sent back down the line.

00:38:26.070 --> 00:38:28.483
And Daz has drawn the short straw.

00:38:28.483 --> 00:38:32.400
(upbeat rock music continues)

00:38:35.610 --> 00:38:37.770
- Basically what's happened
here is the last stag party,

00:38:37.770 --> 00:38:38.910
like some stag parties do,

00:38:38.910 --> 00:38:42.060
decide to treat wor
trains, leave them a tip

00:38:42.060 --> 00:38:43.260
and it's just disgusting, this.

00:38:43.260 --> 00:38:45.720
I mean, these passengers,
eight passengers,

00:38:45.720 --> 00:38:48.020
maybe ten will have to
sit in these seats now.

00:38:49.380 --> 00:38:51.273
I'm just gonna try and
get what I can off here

00:38:51.273 --> 00:38:53.040
just to make it look presentable at least.

00:38:53.040 --> 00:38:54.750
But I should, I've got no tape

00:38:54.750 --> 00:38:56.700
or I'd normally just tape the seat off.

00:38:57.943 --> 00:38:59.160
But I mean, look at this, man.

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:01.680
It's unacceptable behaviour, isn't it?

00:39:01.680 --> 00:39:03.030
If you did this on an airline,

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:05.070
you'd get to your destination
and you'd be arrested.

00:39:05.070 --> 00:39:07.410
Simple as that because that's
what they do on the airlines.

00:39:07.410 --> 00:39:08.910
But we have to put up with it.

00:39:11.460 --> 00:39:12.510
I stink of booze.

00:39:12.510 --> 00:39:15.360
Somebody will probably complain
that I've been drinking.

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:17.543
Nowt we can do, we'll
just have to get it away.

00:39:20.190 --> 00:39:21.900
I haven't been drinking, mind.

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:24.107
I want to today, after all that.

00:39:25.319 --> 00:39:27.518
(Daz blowing whistle)

00:39:27.518 --> 00:39:31.247
(weary music)
(destination board clacking)

00:39:31.247 --> 00:39:32.580
- [Narrator] And at long last,

00:39:32.580 --> 00:39:34.770
there's good news from the control centre

00:39:34.770 --> 00:39:37.008
about the north-running trains.

00:39:37.008 --> 00:39:39.780
- 020, the 14:00 on the
Kings Cross Aberdeen

00:39:39.780 --> 00:39:41.880
is just arriving in platform two.

00:39:41.880 --> 00:39:43.290
So that should clear as many

00:39:43.290 --> 00:39:45.780
northbound passengers as possible.

00:39:45.780 --> 00:39:50.430
Trains will be running
normally between Newcastle

00:39:50.430 --> 00:39:53.163
and Berwick in a few minutes.

00:39:54.219 --> 00:39:57.969
(low bustling of passengers)

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:01.890
- [Narrator] Finally, the whole
line is up and running again

00:40:01.890 --> 00:40:05.190
and Daz can see light at
the end of the tunnel.

00:40:05.190 --> 00:40:07.230
- The way things are going, by about 7:00

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:09.960
we should have most passengers
away by then, you know.

00:40:09.960 --> 00:40:11.250
So it should be good.

00:40:11.250 --> 00:40:12.480
We're getting there now.
We're getting there.

00:40:12.480 --> 00:40:14.639
Slowly but surely, we're
starting to get there, yeah.

00:40:14.639 --> 00:40:16.330
(horn blaring)

00:40:16.330 --> 00:40:18.079
- We're trying to get to
Edinburgh for a hen do

00:40:18.079 --> 00:40:19.253
and the train's here!

00:40:19.253 --> 00:40:22.067
(hens cheering)

00:40:22.067 --> 00:40:24.984
- We've been waiting two hours now.

00:40:24.984 --> 00:40:27.192
Two hours.
- Two hours

00:40:27.192 --> 00:40:28.900
(hens cheering)

00:40:28.900 --> 00:40:31.290
- The train's here!
- Yeah!

00:40:31.290 --> 00:40:34.143
- [Narrator] But there's one
final spanner in the works.

00:40:35.300 --> 00:40:38.357
- That's... this is the 17:25, this one.

00:40:39.358 --> 00:40:41.936
- So is that Northallerton?

00:40:41.936 --> 00:40:43.920
- No, disregard that.
- Okay, yeah.

00:40:43.920 --> 00:40:46.500
- It's a glitch, alright.
That's the 17:25.

00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:48.480
- [Narrator] The controller might say yes,

00:40:48.480 --> 00:40:51.360
but the computer says no.

00:40:51.360 --> 00:40:52.530
- He's gonna try and update it.

00:40:52.530 --> 00:40:54.810
Somehow the computer thinks
it's a different train.

00:40:54.810 --> 00:40:55.943
- Does this stop at Stevenage?

00:40:55.943 --> 00:40:57.870
- It will be. It's the 17:25, sir.

00:40:57.870 --> 00:41:01.035
I'm getting them to do
something about that, okay?

00:41:01.035 --> 00:41:03.393
Howay, little man, you all right, mate?

00:41:05.520 --> 00:41:06.353
I'll just push you to the back there

00:41:06.353 --> 00:41:07.680
for a couple of seconds.

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:09.360
Alright, darling?

00:41:09.360 --> 00:41:10.950
Let me off 'cause I haven't got a ticket.

00:41:10.950 --> 00:41:14.244
Believe it or not, I still
have to pay for it, yeah.

00:41:14.244 --> 00:41:15.710
- Is this the 17:25?
- It is sir, yes.

00:41:15.710 --> 00:41:17.098
Yes, 17:25.

00:41:17.098 --> 00:41:19.827
(upbeat music)

00:41:19.827 --> 00:41:20.933
(door clunks)

00:41:20.933 --> 00:41:23.780
(whistle blows)

00:41:23.780 --> 00:41:26.863
(carriages rumbling)

00:41:32.400 --> 00:41:34.290
- [Narrator] After an especially hectic

00:41:34.290 --> 00:41:38.100
Friday evening shift,
no-one deserves the relief

00:41:38.100 --> 00:41:41.432
of a weekend break more than Daz.

00:41:41.432 --> 00:41:43.110
- We've got everything back to normal.

00:41:43.110 --> 00:41:44.880
We're really happy,
everybody's buzzing again.

00:41:44.880 --> 00:41:47.160
Aye, we'll see the rest
of the night out now.

00:41:47.160 --> 00:41:48.600
And I'll be off for a
drink myself tonight,

00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:51.433
and take my good lady for
something to eat and that, yeah.

00:41:51.433 --> 00:41:52.576
It's payday, you know.

00:41:52.576 --> 00:41:55.500
You've gotta dae it on
payday, eh. (laughs)

00:41:55.500 --> 00:41:57.450
- [Narrator] With happy passengers finally

00:41:57.450 --> 00:41:59.460
on the move from Newcastle,
(destination board clacking)

00:41:59.460 --> 00:42:02.280
back in York there's one last surprise

00:42:02.280 --> 00:42:05.343
for one of the station's
longest serving staff members.

00:42:06.901 --> 00:42:09.150
- [Announcement] On behalf of East Coast

00:42:09.150 --> 00:42:11.947
and everybody at York station,

00:42:11.947 --> 00:42:16.727
we would like to wish JR
a very happy retirement.

00:42:16.727 --> 00:42:19.457
(colleagues applauding)

00:42:19.457 --> 00:42:21.479
- It's a sad day for me.

00:42:21.479 --> 00:42:23.168
Thank you very much.

00:42:23.168 --> 00:42:25.255
Oh, it's a sad day.

00:42:25.255 --> 00:42:26.464
Thank you very much.

00:42:26.464 --> 00:42:28.499
Cheers. Thank you.

00:42:28.499 --> 00:42:32.260
(colleagues applauding)
(upbeat music)

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:34.335
Oh, I'm upset now.
- You alright?

00:42:34.335 --> 00:42:35.461
- I am happy though.

00:42:35.461 --> 00:42:37.479
Thank you all very much.

00:42:37.479 --> 00:42:38.484
Thank you.

00:42:38.484 --> 00:42:42.128
Oh, thank you. Thank you very much.

00:42:42.128 --> 00:42:43.564
Cheers. Thank you all.

00:42:43.564 --> 00:42:44.850
Thank you.

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:46.867
Great. Thank you very much.

00:42:46.867 --> 00:42:48.330
Thank you. Cheers.

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:50.220
I won't forget you as long as I live.

00:42:50.220 --> 00:42:51.693
Thank you very much.

00:42:51.693 --> 00:42:54.535
Thank you, Paola.
- Bye. (kisses)

00:42:54.535 --> 00:42:56.011
- Oh, that's great.

00:42:56.011 --> 00:42:57.548
- Come on then.

00:42:57.548 --> 00:42:58.986
- Hey, thank you.

00:42:58.986 --> 00:43:00.800
- JR has absolutely loved it.

00:43:00.800 --> 00:43:02.610
He didn't expect any of the little

00:43:02.610 --> 00:43:04.470
surprises that we gave him.

00:43:04.470 --> 00:43:09.303
We've given him a nice
sendoff and we'll see him.

00:43:11.160 --> 00:43:12.210
We'll look after him.

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:15.833
- See you later. Bye-bye.

00:43:16.917 --> 00:43:21.298
(upbeat rock and roll music)

00:43:21.298 --> 00:43:22.131
- Bye!

00:43:23.495 --> 00:43:24.328
Oh!

00:43:27.987 --> 00:43:28.820
Aw!

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:34.813
(upbeat rock and roll music continues)

00:43:43.882 --> 00:43:48.465
(upbeat rock and roll music continues)

00:43:56.221 --> 00:43:58.471
(no audio)

